- op: discuss  # c3c5
  at: '2026-08-02T02:54:45Z'  # c3c5
  by: agent  # c3c5
  actor: jgstern_agent  # c3c5
  clock: 19  # c3c5
  nonce: c3c5  # c3c5
  message: "pending_validation -> violated (2026-08-01) on the FRESH-SUBSTRATE evidence this item has been waiting for since 2026-06-11. Directed taint cohort run per the criterion on the item: 9 external repos (zoxide, express, SwiftyJSON, spring-petclinic, caddy, apollo-server, alertmanager, pretix, kserve) spanning Go/JS/TS/Python/Java/Swift/Rust, via hypergumbo verify-claims with a project-agnostic claims file whose every source_taint and prohibited_sink_zone was enumerated from the BUILT-IN catalog before use.\n\nINSTRUMENT WAS POSITIVELY CONTROLLED FIRST, and the first control FAILED (L9/L53). A synthetic repo with a deliberate socket.recv -> Path.write_text flow returned ZERO. Two independent defects in my own harness produced that clean zero: the source name recv is itself in ambiguous_names, and the sink was reached via a chained call Path(...).write_text(...) which the analyzer does not emit at all (no write_text node in the map). Rebuilt with module-qualified unambiguous entries (asyncio.start_server -> os.mkdir and a two-hop -> shutil.copy) the control fires at evidence=2. Had I skipped the control I would have run the cohort, collected clean zeros, and reported this invariant SATISFIED.\n\nRESULT, 321 flows across the 9 repos (corrected by hand: my analyzer print had a misaligned TOTAL row):\n  REALIZABLE 268 | PREFIX_OK 13 | LOCAL_FN 31 | SUSPECT_MOD 8 | NONCALL 1\n  POSITIVE CONTROL: 114 flows had sink primitives in ambiguous_names, i.e. they REQUIRED disambiguation to be correct -- so a low FP count here is meaningful rather than vacuous.\n\nTHE INVARIANT IS VIOLATED ON CLAUSE (a) -- paths must be realizable, every edge corresponding to actual data flow rather than call reachability. Indisputable instances:\n  - pretix javascript d3.v6.js:14596-14606 log (a LOGARITHM function) matched as a console.log LOGGING sink.\n  - pretix javascript d3-dispatch.v2.js:72-78 get (an internal accessor) matched as an http.get NETWORK sink.\n  - kserve go net/http/httptest NewRequest -- the TEST request constructor, which performs no network IO -- matched as a net/http NETWORK sink (x2).\n  - caddy go net/http/fcgi Get matched net/http Get (x2); alertmanager go net Close matched net/smtp.Client Close.\n  - alertmanager go.opentelemetry.io/otel/exporters/otlp/otlptrace/otlptracegrpc NewClient matched the grpc NewClient entry because the module path ENDS in otlptracegrpc.\n  - express javascript lib/application.js:17 debug, a local VARIABLE (var debug = require(debug)(...)), matched console.debug: module AND kind both disagree, and there is no call at all.\n\nROOT CAUSE OF THE GO/SUSPECT_MOD CLASS, confirmed by reading the code: io_boundary._module_matches (io_boundary.py:1538) normalizes :: and / to . and then does BIDIRECTIONAL CASE-INSENSITIVE SUBSTRING matching -- return cm in em or em in cm. So catalog net/http normalizes to net.http, edge net/http/fcgi to net.http.fcgi, and net.http is a substring of it. 25 of 210 catalog sink modules are 4 chars or fewer (os, io, fs, net, log, http, sys, std, file, json, ...), so each substring-matches ANY module containing it: verified live that os matches chaos, io matches audio, log matches dialog, fs matches dfs.client. WI-razol gated ambiguous bare names and ABSENT module hints; it never covered a hint that is PRESENT but matches spuriously. That is why the fix looked complete.\n\nSCOPE OF THE IMPACT, stated narrowly: within the catalog only ONE name/module pair can flip the ZONE verdict (scala run: sys.process.Process host_fs vs sys.process.ProcessBuilder subprocess, where Process is literally a substring of ProcessBuilder). The real exposure is over-matching against arbitrary REPO module names, which is unbounded, plus wrong primitive attribution.\n\nWHY SEVEN WEEKS OF SELF-TREE EVIDENCE MISSED IT: a supplementary run of the same generic claims against hypergumbo itself produced 110 flows, 110 REALIZABLE, 0 suspect, with the disambiguation gate exercised 20 times and correct EVERY time. The fix is genuinely sound on Python. Every defect above is Go or JavaScript. This is a LANGUAGE-COVERAGE gap, not a broken fix -- and it is exactly what the 2026-06-11 note anticipated when it refused to close on self-tree numbers (WI-razol 5975 to 1266) and demanded fresh substrate.\n\nClause (c) -- structural results labeled approximate -- is UPHELD: every run emitted the INV-javam disclosure naming the languages with no taint catalog and stating that claims touching them are not actually verified. Clause (b) (sanitizer neutralization) was NOT measured by this cohort and is not claimed either way.\n\nConcrete fix filed separately. Keeping awaits_bakeoff_validation OFF now: this is no longer an unvalidated claim awaiting a cohort, it is a measured violation with a named root cause."  # c3c5
